{% comment %}
  Oberndoerfer Milano - THE ORNAMENT REVEAL.

  The screen opens as a field of the house mark on the house ground, holds,
  and then dissolves into the site.

  It first shipped as a burst - 96 marks flying outward on individual vectors.
  The founder saw the still field and preferred it, and they were right: the
  burst read as an effect, the still field reads as a house. So the marks do
  not move at all. Nothing travels; the whole field simply fades.

  That judgement also made it much cheaper. There are no per-tile animations
  and no will-change, so the browser composites ONE fading layer instead of 96
  moving ones. If the burst is ever wanted back it needs per-tile
  --x/--y/--dist/--d plus a translate keyframe - see git history rather than
  reconstructing it from memory.

  THE MARK (2026-08-22). The first mask had ROUNDED tips and was wrong. The
  founder sent the real artwork - the points are sharp. Two versions of the
  ornament exist and they are NOT interchangeable:
    - the header wordmark (MILANO_13_x_4_cm_-6.svg) draws it as an OUTLINE
      with softened tips;
    - scratchpad mark.svg, the vector master built from the founder's own PDF,
      has the correct sharp geometry.
  The mask in use is derived from the vector master: rendered at 2400px, the
  outline flood-filled from outside to solidify it, squared, then resampled to
  512 with Lanczos so the points stay crisp. Do not regenerate it from the
  wordmark - the tips come out rounded. The superseded rounded mask is still
  in Files as obm-ornament-mask.png (MediaImage 74278287311173), unused.

  VARIED MARKS (2026-08-22, founder picked option B over adding a wordmark).
  A perfectly uniform field reads as a CSS tile; printed damask does not. Each
  mark therefore carries its own opacity between 0.09 and 0.22.

  WHY THE VALUES ARE A BAKED TABLE AND NOT COMPUTED. The obvious approach - a
  linear congruential hash of the tile index - fails visibly on a regular
  grid, and both failure modes were checked before landing this:
    - a small multiplier (9301) leaves idx*m below the modulus for the first
      ~25 tiles, so the opening two rows RAMP smoothly instead of scattering;
    - tuning the multiplier for maximum neighbour contrast (107610 scored
      best) produces a clean DIAGONAL repeat, because every row is just the
      previous row shifted - worse, since it reads as deliberate striping.
  Liquid has no bitwise operations to mix with, so the table below was solved
  offline instead: an even spread of all 14 levels, then simulated annealing
  against a neighbour-contrast cost until no mark sits within one level of any
  horizontal or vertical neighbour (final cost 0). Deterministic, identical on
  every load, and free at runtime.

  The table is laid out for the 12-column desktop grid. On mobile the grid is
  6 columns, so the pairing differs - that only reshuffles which values land
  beside each other, and since the table has no structure to expose there is
  nothing to degrade.

  ONCE PER SESSION, not per page load. A customer opens many pages in a visit,
  and a curtain on each one stops being a welcome and becomes a toll gate. It
  plays on the first page of a session; every page after keeps the quiet
  entrance in oberndoerfer-global-styles instead.

  ?reveal=1 FORCES it, ignoring the session gate, and deliberately does NOT set
  the seen-flag. Without it the thing is nearly impossible to review: the gate
  arms itself on the first load after any deploy, so if you blink you cannot
  see it again without a brand-new browser session.

  Fails safe in three ways, because a stuck curtain would hide the whole shop:
    1. The dismissal is a pure CSS animation with `forwards`, so it runs even
       if JavaScript never executes. JS only decides whether to SKIP it.
    2. pointer-events stay none for its whole life, so it can never swallow a
       click even if it somehow stays painted.
    3. It is position:fixed and outside the layout, so it cannot shift content
       or cause CLS.

  If sessionStorage throws (private mode, storage blocked) the catch does
  nothing, so the curtain SHOWS and the CSS dismisses it. Failing toward
  "plays too often" rather than "never dismisses" is the safe direction.

  The marks are applied as a mask so the shape takes its colour from CSS - one
  cached request for the whole field, and the opacity variation above is
  possible at all because each tile is a coloured box, not an image.

  The grid is expressed in fractions of the viewport, not fixed pixels, so 96
  tiles cover any screen from a phone to a 5K display without gaps and without
  generating hundreds of nodes.
{% endcomment %}

<script>
  (function () {
    if (/[?&]reveal=1(&|$)/.test(location.search)) return; /* forced: always play */
    try {
      if (sessionStorage.getItem('obm-reveal-seen')) {
        document.documentElement.classList.add('obm-reveal-seen');
      } else {
        sessionStorage.setItem('obm-reveal-seen', '1');
      }
    } catch (e) {
      /* storage unavailable: show it and let the CSS dismiss it */
    }
  })();
</script>

<style>
  .obm-reveal {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 9999;
    background-color: #f5f2ed;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    grid-auto-rows: calc(100vh / 8);
    animation: obm-curtain-out 1100ms cubic-bezier(0.33, 1, 0.68, 1) forwards;
  }

  .obm-reveal__tile {
    place-self: center;
    width: 44%;
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    background-color: rgba(26, 22, 18, var(--a, 0.16));
    -webkit-mask-image: url("https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0758/8387/2581/files/obm-ornament-mask-v2.png?v=1787389775");
    mask-image: url("https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0758/8387/2581/files/obm-ornament-mask-v2.png?v=1787389775");
    -webkit-mask-size: contain;
    mask-size: contain;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    -webkit-mask-position: center;
    mask-position: center;
  }

  /* the field holds, then the whole thing dissolves - nothing travels */
  @keyframes obm-curtain-out {
    0%,
    36% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    100% {
      opacity: 0;
      visibility: hidden;
    }
  }

  /* seen already this session - never paint it at all */
  html.obm-reveal-seen .obm-reveal {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .obm-reveal {
      animation: obm-curtain-out 420ms ease-out forwards;
    }
  }

  @media screen and (max-width: 749px) {
    .obm-reveal {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(6, 1fr);
      grid-auto-rows: calc(100vh / 12);
    }
  }
</style>

{%- liquid
  # opacity x100 per mark - solved offline, see the comment above
  assign shades = "19,12,17,13,15,18,15,13,11,9,15,22,17,14,9,16,13,15,11,9,13,18,21,18,20,16,21,19,21,13,16,11,9,22,19,10,11,13,16,14,19,10,22,20,14,18,11,15,21,9,22,10,14,22,19,12,20,9,17,20,10,21,15,21,10,18,11,16,12,17,12,18,14,16,19,14,16,20,17,11,20,10,17,12,9,19,14,17,22,12,10,20,13,18,12,15" | split: ","
-%}
<div class="obm-reveal" aria-hidden="true" role="presentation">
  {%- for i in (1..96) -%}
    {%- assign a = shades[forloop.index0] | times: 1 | divided_by: 100.0 -%}
    <span class="obm-reveal__tile" style="--a:{{ a }}"></span>
  {%- endfor -%}
</div>
